/** Standalone batch V -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT uva-288 and uva-297 whose official Samples turned out to be corrupted (see below).
 *
 * uva-255-correct-move: king moves orthogonally by 1 (never onto the queen); queen slides orthogonally
 *   blocked by the king. "Allowed" = legal AND not also reachable by the other piece from the current
 *   state. After an allowed queen move, the king is "locked in" (Stop) iff it has zero allowed moves in
 *   the resulting state. Verified against the problem's own worked example (king=17,queen=49) including
 *   its explicit "Move not allowed" case (place 25, reachable by both pieces).
 * uva-263-number-chains: at each step, take the CURRENT value's own decimal digits (digit count can
 *   shrink between steps, e.g. leading zeros collapsing on the ascending arrangement), sort ascending
 *   and descending, subtract; stop when a value repeats, chain length = count of distinct values
 *   including the start. Verified against Kaprekar's constant (6174) both as a produced intermediate
 *   value and as a starting value (immediate self-loop, chain length 1).
 * uva-264-count-on-cantor: term k lies on diagonal d where triangular(d-1) < k <= triangular(d), at
 *   position p = k - triangular(d-1) within it; even diagonals count num=p,den=d+1-p, odd diagonals
 *   count the reverse -- verified directly against the problem's own diagram (terms 1..5).
 * uva-288-arithmetic-operations-with-large-integers: THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE IS CORRUPTED -- it
 *   preserves the source PDF's own cosmetic 75-char line-wrapping (with a literal "\\" two-backslash
 *   continuation marker) as if it were real expected output, even though the problem statement itself
 *   says the wrapping is only "for visual reasons" and the true output "must be just a line"; the
 *   sample also inserts a spurious blank line between every case that the statement never mentions.
 *   Confirmed by dewrapping the sample (strip blank lines, rejoin "\\"-continued lines) and diffing
 *   against this solver on the sample's own input -- every case matched exactly once dewrapped. That
 *   same dewrapping also caught a real solver bug: "**" chains (e.g. "4**3**2**1") must be evaluated
 *   RIGHT-associative (4**(3**(2**1))=4**9=262144), not left-associative -- fixed and reverified.
 *   sampleLimit: 0, replaced with hand-verified data below. JS BigInt handles the arbitrary-precision
 *   arithmetic natively (no custom bignum needed) since it has no built-in size limit.
 * uva-294-divisors: for each range, the number with the most divisors (ties -> lowest). The stress
 *   case's expected output was computed via a segmented sieve (primes up to sqrt(1e9)~31623, sweeping
 *   each range by prime multiples) rather than the boundary/tricky cases' simple trial-division, since
 *   naive trial division at stress scale (200 ranges * 10001 width * sqrt(1e9)) is ~10^10 operations
 *   and impractically slow to even GENERATE; the fast solver was cross-checked byte-for-byte against
 *   the slow one on the boundary and tricky inputs before being trusted at stress scale.
 * uva-297-quadtrees: THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE IS ALSO CORRUPTED -- an order-independent area-sum
 *   parse (which MUST always sum to a whole number of pixels for any valid quadtree of a 1024-pixel
 *   canvas, regardless of node order) produces fractional totals like "500.328125" for several of the
 *   sample's own quadtree strings, and a max-depth-only parse independently confirms some of those
 *   strings nest 'p' up to 8 levels deep -- structurally impossible for a 32x32 image, whose leaves
 *   must bottom out by depth 5. Almost certainly stray/duplicated characters from scraping. The
 *   standard depth<=5, 32x32 recursive-quadrant-fill parser below still matches 10 of the sample's 15
 *   cases despite the corruption in the other 5. sampleLimit: 0, replaced with hand-verified data below.
 * uva-300-maya-calendar: Haab day-of-year (month_index*20+day, uayet is month 18 with 0-4) plus
 *   year*365 gives an absolute day count from day 0 = "1 imix 0"; Tzolkin number = day%13+1 (1-indexed,
 *   cycling from 1), name = TZOLKIN_NAMES[day%20], year = floor(day/260) (the 13*20=260-day Tzolkin
 *   year). Verified directly against the problem statement's own day-0 example.
 */
